WEBVTT

NOTE Module 03 transcript — auto-aligned to narration.wav (127.7s). en.

1
00:00:00.164 --> 00:00:06.595
Welcome to Module Three — convolutional neural networks, the architecture that made deep learning practical for images.

2
00:00:06.956 --> 00:00:12.969
We'll cover why convolutions beat dense layers, the building blocks, the classic architectures, and transfer learning.

3
00:00:13.323 --> 00:00:22.280
The convolution itself is simple: a small kernel slides across the image, applying the same weights at every position.

4
00:00:22.649 --> 00:00:32.232
That weight sharing is the whole trick — it gives you translation equivariance and a tiny fraction of the parameters a dense layer would need.

5
00:00:32.592 --> 00:00:39.848
The number of filters sets your output channels, and the receptive field grows as you stack layers deeper.

6
00:00:40.193 --> 00:00:43.093
Between convolutions you downsample.

7
00:00:43.425 --> 00:00:52.114
Max pooling keeps the strongest response in a window, average pooling smooths, and a strided convolution does the same thing but learns how.

8
00:00:52.481 --> 00:00:57.715
In modern nets, global average pooling quietly replaces the old dense classifier head.

9
00:00:58.082 --> 00:01:02.029
The architecture story is a quick tour.

10
00:01:02.362 --> 00:01:03.029
LeNet-5 in 'ninety-eight.

11
00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:08.927
AlexNet in twenty-twelve, which added ReLU, GPUs, and dropout to win ImageNet.

12
00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:12.321
VGG went deeper with stacked three-by-three convolutions.

13
00:01:12.683 --> 00:01:17.003
ResNet added skip connections and suddenly a hundred-plus layers trained cleanly.

14
00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:19.428
EfficientNet and ConvNeXt are the modern refinements.

15
00:01:19.774 --> 00:01:23.262
Here's the move you'll use most: transfer learning.

16
00:01:23.605 --> 00:01:31.301
Start from ImageNet weights, freeze the base, and train a small head on your own data.

17
00:01:31.654 --> 00:01:41.615
Then unfreeze and fine-tune with a much smaller learning rate — and match your preprocessing to the pretrained model, keeping batch-norm layers in inference mode at first.

18
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:46.943
And almost for free, data augmentation regularizes you.

19
00:01:47.315 --> 00:01:53.194
Random flips, crops, and rotations are built into Keras; MixUp, CutMix, and RandAugment go further.

20
00:01:53.565 --> 00:02:00.981
Just apply it during training only, and lean on it harder when your dataset is small.

21
00:02:01.318 --> 00:02:07.121
Let's build and visualize a CNN — open the labs.
